#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151889 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "101e0c22-fbb4-5865-af5f-d669e2488d09" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Linear ODEs Using the Superposition Principle"}] :question/id #uuid "3a4c4912-e478-5332-b088-0df7ac4e8c42" :question/math-academy-id "e-6578" :question/problem "Given that $y_1(x) = e^{-x}$ and $y_2(x) = e^{3x}$ are solutions to the equation $y'' - 2y - 3y = 0,$ which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x) = 7e^{-x} - 6e^{3x}$\n\n- $y(x) = \\dfrac{e^{3x}}{e^{-x}}$\n\n- $y(x) =- e^{x} + e^{3x}$" :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and $c_1$ and $c_2$ are constants, then $y(x) = c_1 y_1(x) + c_2 y_2(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nWe're given that $y_1(x) = e^{-x}$ and $y_2(x) =e^{3x}.$ Therefore, the following function is also a solution:\n\n$$\ny(t) = c_1 e^{-x} + c_2 e^{3x}\n$$\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x) = 7e^{-x} - 6e^{3x}$ is a solution of the given equation. Here, $c_1 = 7$ and $c_2 = -6.$\n\n- The function $y(x) = \\dfrac{e^{3x}}{e^{-x}}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the given equation. The superposition principle only applies to linear combinations of known solutions. It does not extend to the divisions of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x) = - e^{x} + e^{3x}$  is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the given equation because $e^x$ is not a solution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151923 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "42bc95a3-4e0c-5487-8427-31cee0ecf80b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions of Linear ODEs Using the Sum Rule"}] :question/id #uuid "7e533704-ef66-5bab-b151-83345bce320b" :question/math-academy-id "e-6865" :question/problem "Given that $y_1(x) = e^{-2x}$ and $y_2(x) = e^x$ are solutions to the equation $y''+y'-2y=0,$ then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x) = \\dfrac{e^{-2x}}{e^{x}}$\n\n- $y(x) = e^{-2x}\\cdot e^{x}$\n\n- $y(x) = e^{-2x} +e^{x}$" :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous differential equation, then $y(x) = y_1(x) + y_2(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x) = \\dfrac{e^{-2x}}{e^{x}}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the quotients of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x) = e^{-2x}\\cdot e^{x}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the products of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x) =  e^{-2x} +e^{x}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151924 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0829d4a-239a-5a8e-9c8e-3cdc76a993bc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions of Linear ODEs Using the Constant Multiple Rule"}] :question/id #uuid "c4c3ed32-67c8-56a3-9855-4bfa2696bfc9" :question/math-academy-id "e-6866" :question/problem "Given that $y_1(x) = e^{2x}$ is a solution to the equation $y'' - 4y = 0,$ then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x) = 2xe^{2x}$\n\n- $y(x) = 3e^{2x}$\n\n- $y(x) = -e^{2x}$" :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the function $y_1(x)$ is a solution to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and $c$ is a constant, then $y(x) = c y_1(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x) = 2xe^{2x}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of a non-constant function and a known solution.\n\n- The function $y(x) = 3e^{2x}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c=3.$\n\n- The function $y(x) = -e^{2x}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c=-1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388631 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "42bc95a3-4e0c-5487-8427-31cee0ecf80b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions of Linear ODEs Using the Sum Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97a4c0b0-f3de-5b28-981e-2234e33e316b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388633} {:answer/id #uuid "a2ec5fcc-f4f0-5b60-abf7-66b387fc510d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388634} {:answer/id #uuid "163fd82e-824e-541e-ba58-607ebf4b9af4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388635} {:answer/id #uuid "3747e8e6-b9e4-5038-8bc8-36a0ad39de46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388636} {:answer/id #uuid "373737a2-fdd8-5002-90c4-e01010989661" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388637}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "373737a2-fdd8-5002-90c4-e01010989661" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388637} :answer-field/id #uuid "7bee083f-fb40-5275-a3fc-8147377d1d9d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388632}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a02f4b33-a5b8-5f4c-9305-d8176d9840e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-131013" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-3x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{4x}$ are solutions to the equation ${y}^{″}-{y}^{′}-12y=0,$ then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x)=\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{{e}^{-3x}}$\n- $y(x)={e}^{4x}+{e}^{-3x}$\n- $y(x)={e}^{x}$" :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, then $y(x)={y}_{1}(x)+{y}_{2}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{{e}^{-3x}}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the quotients of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)={e}^{4x}+{e}^{-3x}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle.\n\n- The function $y(x)={e}^{x}={e}^{4x}\\cdot {e}^{-3x}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the products of the known solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343804 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "42bc95a3-4e0c-5487-8427-31cee0ecf80b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions of Linear ODEs Using the Sum Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "662d1d06-dd17-5de3-83ec-2cedc1760998" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343806} {:answer/id #uuid "d949191f-9f46-55e9-9520-7e4e12abf55e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343807} {:answer/id #uuid "8663551c-ab1d-55f1-97d0-860ea6a112c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343808} {:answer/id #uuid "9bf3db7b-9abf-5af5-bcdf-e17ab7417764" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343809} {:answer/id #uuid "23cca72b-25f8-5148-9741-215c615b35b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of these listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343810}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8663551c-ab1d-55f1-97d0-860ea6a112c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343808} :answer-field/id #uuid "24817143-3fcf-5599-bdca-2bb39117ba27" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343805}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9075355b-7512-5362-8a9e-57357160d013" :question/math-academy-id "q-131014" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{2}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={x}^{4}$ are solutions to the equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-5x{y}^{′}+8y=0,$ then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x)={x}^{6}$\n- $y(x)={x}^{2}+4x$\n- $y(x)={x}^{2}(1+{x}^{2})$" :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, then $y(x)={y}_{1}(x)+{y}_{2}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)={x}^{6}={x}^{2}\\cdot {x}^{4}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the products of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)={x}^{2}+4x$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle because $4x$ is not one of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)={x}^{2}(1+{x}^{2})={x}^{2}+{x}^{4}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388638 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0829d4a-239a-5a8e-9c8e-3cdc76a993bc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions of Linear ODEs Using the Constant Multiple Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c73d9b4-d20a-5dc8-a807-39dd4ca925fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388640} {:answer/id #uuid "5ba70b58-63f8-529c-971d-f86a0e16f589" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388641} {:answer/id #uuid "f0fdd4c9-acbe-5997-8ccc-436971a7c379" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388642} {:answer/id #uuid "f2e2e5ea-2b58-5561-b741-7c24c98a9f9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388643} {:answer/id #uuid "6413f2a0-6ea1-5c9a-8dc0-7f9c41840497" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388644}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f2e2e5ea-2b58-5561-b741-7c24c98a9f9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388643} :answer-field/id #uuid "571a1259-2474-51be-819c-5bacde8bc540" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388639}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "51bd1b14-4e3d-59b8-aceb-0dbcc911c5cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-131020" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x$ is a solution to the equation ${y}^{″}+9y=0,$ then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x$\n- $y(x)=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x$\n- $y(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x$" :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the function ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and $c$ is a constant, then $y(x)=c{y}_{1}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c=-3.$\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c=\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\n- The function $y(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of a non-constant function and a known solution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388645 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0829d4a-239a-5a8e-9c8e-3cdc76a993bc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions of Linear ODEs Using the Constant Multiple Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0588687c-c693-584f-bc4c-3549d000d3d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388647} {:answer/id #uuid "0756c3a4-35c8-5711-ad57-4579fc25c723" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388648} {:answer/id #uuid "e4017307-a832-54e6-bc2c-dc51aa3c6d3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388649} {:answer/id #uuid "418ec1c5-491b-5b2b-be73-945bb782b374" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388650} {:answer/id #uuid "c778259f-1272-5d91-8e86-459694de1c83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388651}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e4017307-a832-54e6-bc2c-dc51aa3c6d3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388649} :answer-field/id #uuid "7911dc6f-02db-56ba-930a-f61f1029be93" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388646}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2081eb5b-57d7-57e6-a013-e3e8bcd2b543" :question/math-academy-id "q-131022" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{4}$ is a solution to the equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-7x{y}^{′}+16y=0,$ then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x)=-2{x}^{4}$\n- $y(x)={x}^{4}\\sqrt{x}$\n- $y(x)=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{e}^{x}}$" :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the function ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and $c$ is a constant, then $y(x)=c{y}_{1}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)=-2{x}^{4}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c=-2.$\n\n- The function $y(x)={x}^{4}\\sqrt{x}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of a non-constant function and a known solution.\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{e}^{x}}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the quotient of a known solution and a non-constant function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388652 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "101e0c22-fbb4-5865-af5f-d669e2488d09" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Linear ODEs Using the Superposition Principle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "92e14436-e144-5ede-9456-3da664822f83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{3{x}^{4}}{{x}^{5}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388654} {:answer/id #uuid "59d0464b-2411-57a9-8ac9-4a7147906065" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={x}^{5}+2{x}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388655} {:answer/id #uuid "9f469a90-8161-544a-90e3-55196efe1ccf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2{x}^{9}+3}{{x}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388656} {:answer/id #uuid "c5472481-624f-54f3-87e4-e5532015917d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{x}^{5}+2}{2{x}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388657} {:answer/id #uuid "90572c68-9fcd-55ae-95f0-6e24f81b49f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={x}^{5}+2{x}^{4}+{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388658}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f469a90-8161-544a-90e3-55196efe1ccf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2{x}^{9}+3}{{x}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388656} :answer-field/id #uuid "016916d9-eaee-5c25-a2a8-06d1c47f0da5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388653}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "93ee6e63-1b10-5325-b502-afabadb6eb8e" :question/math-academy-id "q-131029" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{5}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}$ are solutions to the equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-20y=0,$ what function must also be a solution to the given differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ are constants, then $y(x)={c}_{1}{y}_{1}(x)+{c}_{2}{y}_{2}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nWe're given that ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{5}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}.$ Therefore, the following function is also a solution:\n\n$$\ny(x)={c}_{1}{x}^{5}+{c}_{2}\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}\n$$\n\nIf we let ${c}_{1}=2$ and ${c}_{2}=3,$ we get:\n\n$$\ny(x)=2{x}^{5}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}}=\\frac{2{x}^{9}+3}{{x}^{4}}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343825 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "101e0c22-fbb4-5865-af5f-d669e2488d09" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Linear ODEs Using the Superposition Principle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fad8b97e-924a-588f-ae21-578df2180d33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-x}+{e}^{x}+{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343827} {:answer/id #uuid "2e641187-eb10-5851-8598-faedcd00898a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x{e}^{x}+{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343828} {:answer/id #uuid "5bbd4043-78d5-5143-90d7-14b147500f43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-2x}}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343829} {:answer/id #uuid "370ef8d7-7a1c-5d5c-bf91-c561f2638815" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2{e}^{-x}+{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343830} {:answer/id #uuid "82eba82e-6539-54f0-8c8c-5f3c78dee6fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2{e}^{x}-{e}^{-2x}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343831}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82eba82e-6539-54f0-8c8c-5f3c78dee6fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2{e}^{x}-{e}^{-2x}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343831} :answer-field/id #uuid "597072c7-e9da-5af9-b902-e0614d3bb99d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343826}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5c0ea85a-8646-5af0-8c5d-b95cfa962333" :question/math-academy-id "q-131032" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-2x}$ are solutions to the equation ${y}^{″}+{y}^{′}-2y=0,$ what function must also be a solution to the given differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ are constants, then $y(x)={c}_{1}{y}_{1}(x)+{c}_{2}{y}_{2}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nWe're given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-2x}.$ Therefore, the following function is also a solution:\n\n$$\ny(x)={c}_{1}{e}^{x}+{c}_{2}{e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nIf we let ${c}_{1}=\\frac{2}{3}$ and ${c}_{2}=-\\frac{1}{3},$ we get:\n\n$$\ny(x)=\\frac{2{e}^{x}}{3}-\\frac{{e}^{-2x}}{3}=\\frac{2{e}^{x}-{e}^{-2x}}{3}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388659 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "101e0c22-fbb4-5865-af5f-d669e2488d09" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Linear ODEs Using the Superposition Principle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "72c5c4fe-f012-52cf-bde5-408c97208149" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388661} {:answer/id #uuid "00c7d3ab-fb49-5d1b-ae29-52e25e1f95db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388662} {:answer/id #uuid "bd6a854d-53d6-50fc-9107-0f2a59743d35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388663} {:answer/id #uuid "b10a246c-2b73-5575-81a7-4368685878df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388664} {:answer/id #uuid "c80aa570-a716-5577-b61f-eb8cb7f8c12e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388665}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b10a246c-2b73-5575-81a7-4368685878df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388664} :answer-field/id #uuid "02c77a78-348d-5138-8263-d15956b8585f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388660}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f189b852-5b24-5d57-9057-b50373fc4db7" :question/math-academy-id "q-131036" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-x}$ are solutions to the equation ${y}^{″}-y=0,$ which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x)={e}^{x}+4{e}^{-x}$\n- $y(x)=5{e}^{-x}$\n- $y(x)=({e}^{-x}+1){e}^{x}$" :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ are constants, then $y(x)={c}_{1}{y}_{1}(x)+{c}_{2}{y}_{2}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nWe're given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-x}.$ Therefore, the following function is also a solution:\n\n$$\ny(x)={c}_{1}{e}^{x}+{c}_{2}{e}^{-x}\n$$\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)={e}^{x}+4{e}^{-x}$ is a solution of the given equation. Here, ${c}_{1}=1,{c}_{2}=4.$\n\n- The function $y(x)=5{e}^{-x}$ is a solution of the given equation. Here, ${c}_{1}=0,{c}_{2}=5.$\n\n- The function $({e}^{-x}+1){e}^{x}=1+{e}^{x}$ is **not** a solution of the given equation because $1$ is not a solution."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152559 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "101e0c22-fbb4-5865-af5f-d669e2488d09" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Linear ODEs Using the Superposition Principle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b98b7743-50ca-5ac2-ad5c-94f67864def8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-2t^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150265} {:answer/id #uuid "fe66408f-03ed-5776-a795-32c7b4401962" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{t^{2} - 1}{3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150266} {:answer/id #uuid "0f8d8a7b-cb06-59ef-8ae8-2a4e31ccd86d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-3t + 4t^{2} + t^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150267} {:answer/id #uuid "33e4ca12-de43-5bc6-b502-dc7ad5fc62d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{t + 1}{t^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150268} {:answer/id #uuid "7b3c73bf-14df-5a8c-accc-b827f3ce7072" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{t - t^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150269}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b3c73bf-14df-5a8c-accc-b827f3ce7072" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{t - t^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150269} :answer-field/id #uuid "b312ae49-5048-5e22-b49d-8e9c2e81af5c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150264}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "89267386-8848-5cc2-a0aa-aa43b53e1e20" :question/math-academy-id "q-65112" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(t)=t$ and ${y}_{2}(t)={t}^{2}$ are solutions to the equation ${t}^{2}{y}^{″}-2t{y}^{′}+2y=0$ for $t>0,$ what function must also be a solution to the given differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(t)$ and ${y}_{2}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ are constants, then $y(t)={c}_{1}{y}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{y}_{2}(t)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nWe're given that ${y}_{1}(t)=t$ and ${y}_{2}(t)={t}^{2}.$ Therefore, the following function is also a solution:\n\n$$\ny(t)={c}_{1}t+{c}_{2}{t}^{2}\n$$\n\nIf we let ${c}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}$ and ${c}_{2}=-\\frac{1}{2},$ we get:\n\n$$\ny(t)=\\frac{t-{t}^{2}}{2}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152730 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "42bc95a3-4e0c-5487-8427-31cee0ecf80b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions of Linear ODEs Using the Sum Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c7d01b7a-f38c-59f9-97ee-a287e86c9eee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151291} {:answer/id #uuid "e6af5306-df63-5484-b373-30ea7660bac5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151292} {:answer/id #uuid "edaa0045-8c8f-539e-9f73-a46c8eded708" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151293} {:answer/id #uuid "4f2b1275-6b11-56c5-956d-0ad47ec7d74f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151294} {:answer/id #uuid "6253ea5c-710a-564a-accb-bffb0f7bf9ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151295}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "edaa0045-8c8f-539e-9f73-a46c8eded708" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151293} :answer-field/id #uuid "df66d29f-a0b7-556d-8c75-2884bc80727d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151290}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "46b25723-729d-510e-b94e-77440edfe286" :question/math-academy-id "q-79724" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear, second-order ODE, then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x)=(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{2}$\n- $y(x)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$\n- $y(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$" :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, then $y(x)={y}_{1}(x)+{y}_{2}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)=(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{2}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of a function and the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152732 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0829d4a-239a-5a8e-9c8e-3cdc76a993bc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions of Linear ODEs Using the Constant Multiple Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8eb46778-e24c-5a82-a4e5-5514dfabc579" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151303} {:answer/id #uuid "909edee3-8fc0-5a06-b685-2d61381ef0c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151304} {:answer/id #uuid "d225121c-a814-5aab-86f0-8a5911460bef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151305} {:answer/id #uuid "f095d4c5-e90b-53da-837a-a0527a1650bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151306} {:answer/id #uuid "5be73eee-a984-5e11-b02b-7b5c4e25cd8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151307}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f095d4c5-e90b-53da-837a-a0527a1650bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151306} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb38749d-33bc-5c68-b181-087c646fccbc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151302}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f6e0c318-7db3-5a86-afe7-c1d5265937a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-79730" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ is a solution to a homogeneous, linear, second-order ODE, then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x)={sin}^{2}⁡(2x)$\n- $y(x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$\n- $y(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$" :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the function ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and $c$ is a constant, then $y(x)=c{y}_{1}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)={sin}^{2}⁡(2x)$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c=-1.$\n\n- The function $y(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of a non-constant function and a known solution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343860 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0829d4a-239a-5a8e-9c8e-3cdc76a993bc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions of Linear ODEs Using the Constant Multiple Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d72c6e1c-d257-5ad1-93b5-6eb80bc738b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343862} {:answer/id #uuid "bec5a050-34cd-58d0-8449-b9dcabaf82d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343863} {:answer/id #uuid "07c8c2b6-a5e7-53a5-adb2-79404773427d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343864} {:answer/id #uuid "b5209a10-aa98-53b5-895d-ecce39b09eae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343865} {:answer/id #uuid "aaae9815-b868-5a77-aee5-79142c179d66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343866}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aaae9815-b868-5a77-aee5-79142c179d66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343866} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b3bfc3b-6379-5e8d-ba2c-71c72115635f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343861}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7ccb0584-4fe2-575d-b3bf-456e92f83038" :question/math-academy-id "q-79774" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{4x}$ is a solution to the equation ${y}^{″}-2{y}^{′}-8y=0,$ then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x)=4x{e}^{4x}$\n- $y(x)=2{e}^{4x}$\n- $y(x)=\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{x}$" :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the function ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and $c$ is a constant, then $y(x)=c{y}_{1}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)=4x{e}^{4x}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of a non-constant function and a known solution.\n\n- The function $y(x)=2{e}^{4x}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c=2.$\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{x}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of a non-constant function and a known solution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388666 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "101e0c22-fbb4-5865-af5f-d669e2488d09" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Linear ODEs Using the Superposition Principle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "161d0c77-dc99-53a8-ab98-7b8e03d93d86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388668} {:answer/id #uuid "72c418eb-a710-5b46-a94f-2f81ec420088" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388669} {:answer/id #uuid "e8d8e4b5-ca78-56b1-8b37-ddcfbbcba552" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388670} {:answer/id #uuid "f4fd9436-2955-56d8-86f3-8bbf62aabce2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388671} {:answer/id #uuid "5098e556-3303-5972-90ca-d7f8cc586183" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388672}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "72c418eb-a710-5b46-a94f-2f81ec420088" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388669} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6e0db08-a413-5785-95bd-218eebe99cee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388667}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "742f0d7e-0be6-5200-afaf-b211266d03e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-79799" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(t)={e}^{-t}$ and ${y}_{2}(t)={e}^{4t}$ are solutions to the equation ${y}^{″}-3{y}^{′}-4y=0,$ which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(t)={e}^{-t-1}$\n- $y(t)=7{e}^{-t}-3{e}^{-4t}$\n- $y(t)=\\frac{{e}^{4t}}{4}$" :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(t)$ and ${y}_{2}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ are constants, then $y(t)={c}_{1}{y}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{y}_{2}(t)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nWe're given that ${y}_{1}(t)={e}^{-t}$ and ${y}_{2}(t)={e}^{4t}.$ Therefore, the following function is also a solution:\n\n$$\ny(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}\n$$\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(t)={e}^{-t-1}=\\frac{{e}^{-t}}{e}$ is a solution of the given equation. Here, ${c}_{1}=\\frac{1}{e},{c}_{2}=0.$\n\n- The function $y(t)=7{e}^{-t}-3{e}^{-4t}$ is **not** a solution of the given equation because ${e}^{-4t}$ is not a solution.\n\n- The function $y(t)=\\frac{{e}^{4t}}{4}$ is a solution of the given equation. Here, ${c}_{1}=0,{c}_{2}=\\frac{1}{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388673 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "42bc95a3-4e0c-5487-8427-31cee0ecf80b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions of Linear ODEs Using the Sum Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a40d472-8bbf-5caf-8046-8da2b487351d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388675} {:answer/id #uuid "f4f44b7d-1acb-5b45-b122-6a0f3511bcfa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388676} {:answer/id #uuid "26746e79-d2e5-57ea-a50a-f02ab98ebd3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388677} {:answer/id #uuid "7b99d9bc-a613-507a-8b70-c3fd648854bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388678} {:answer/id #uuid "3edb745c-a6fb-5b66-8830-d0d4da293eeb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388679}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3edb745c-a6fb-5b66-8830-d0d4da293eeb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388679} :answer-field/id #uuid "41089653-962d-576d-8c92-e365b16ff75a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388674}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2690375c-0561-512e-b08c-8c068f5f6e49" :question/math-academy-id "q-79800" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{2}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear, second-order ODE, then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x)={x}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$\n- $y(x)={x}^{2}(1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)$\n- $y(x)={x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$" :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, then $y(x)={y}_{1}(x)+{y}_{2}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)={x}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x={x}^{2}\\cdot {x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the quotients of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)={x}^{2}(1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)={x}^{2}+{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle.\n\n- The function $y(x)={x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle because $\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is not one of the known solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388680 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "42bc95a3-4e0c-5487-8427-31cee0ecf80b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions of Linear ODEs Using the Sum Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7cc7520d-9c43-5b9b-bd5c-1653a09ecacb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388682} {:answer/id #uuid "982bc1a6-a598-50fc-bc6d-5312f838b32a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388683} {:answer/id #uuid "c934f1f1-c922-5ad5-b317-553779acd642" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388684} {:answer/id #uuid "79264ba9-31b4-5f55-b8ad-4a1e6217c468" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388685} {:answer/id #uuid "00482635-bebb-5a06-a112-1e469ff33e86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388686}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c934f1f1-c922-5ad5-b317-553779acd642" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388684} :answer-field/id #uuid "27227c8e-ba56-58b3-bd9b-95f9fb9ddab7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388681}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f1c83c9e-49e0-5c44-a338-0361da3bd800" :question/math-academy-id "q-79826" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear, second-order ODE, then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x)=x\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)$\n- $y(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}$\n- $y(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)$" :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, then $y(x)={y}_{1}(x)+{y}_{2}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)=x\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of a function and the known \nsolutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the quotients of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388687 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0829d4a-239a-5a8e-9c8e-3cdc76a993bc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions of Linear ODEs Using the Constant Multiple Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9166bce4-3634-5e1c-8c3e-ed294047e766" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388689} {:answer/id #uuid "1356f09f-12a1-537f-9506-c291fa199140" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388690} {:answer/id #uuid "68d90bc1-c928-59cf-8341-1112ed1a6d27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388691} {:answer/id #uuid "0e319f75-aca9-5e1e-acb6-d495752c0034" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388692} {:answer/id #uuid "8d5fa24e-dc65-5911-9d3b-ed994e2aca76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388693}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d5fa24e-dc65-5911-9d3b-ed994e2aca76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388693} :answer-field/id #uuid "86058da3-cc50-5c8d-9dd0-c1823d49a53a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388688}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "902f8e05-10b0-58cd-8a55-2c894b53c82e" :question/math-academy-id "q-79830" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{2}$ is a solution to the equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}-4y=0,$ then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x)=3{x}^{4}$\n- $y(x)=-{x}^{2}$\n- $y(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$" :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the function ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and $c$ is a constant, then $y(x)=c{y}_{1}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)=3{x}^{4}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle because $y(x)={x}^{4}$ is not a solution.\n\n- The function $y(x)=-{x}^{2}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c=-1.$\n\n- The function $y(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of a non-constant function and a known solution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}]}
